Here’s what was added and how it lines up with the rest of the app.

### `UserProfile` (async server component)

It follows the same shape as `getProducts()` in `page.tsx`: a small async data helper, `fetch` to the app API path, and a catch branch with mock data so builds do not break if the request fails.

```1:28:/vercel/sandbox/app/UserProfile.tsx
interface UserProfileData {
  name: string
  email: string
}

async function getUserProfile(): Promise<UserProfileData> {
  try {
    const res = await fetch('/api/users/profile')
    return res.json()
  } catch {
    return { name: 'Sample User', email: 'user@example.com' }
  }
}

export default async function UserProfile() {
  const user = await getUserProfile()

  return (
    <div>
      <h2>Profile</h2>
      <div>{user.name}</div>
      <div>{user.email}</div>
    </div>
  )
}
```

Presentation matches `ProductList`: a wrapper `div`, an `h2` title, then simple `div`s for each field.

### `/api/users/profile`

There was no `app/api` tree before, so a route handler was added so `GET /api/users/profile` returns JSON the component can use (you can swap this for real data or an upstream fetch later).

```1:7:/vercel/sandbox/app/api/users/profile/route.ts
export async function GET() {
  return Response.json({
    name: 'Alex Morgan',
    email: 'alex@example.com',
  })
}
```

`npm run build` completes successfully with these changes.

